      "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Two companies (Cortical Labs, Melbourne; FinalSpark, Vevey) are commercially selling access to living human neural tissue integrated with silicon chips. Through systematic review of publicly available documentation, this audit establishes that neither company has published consent documentation tracing tissue sourcing to original donors, institutional ethics oversight structures, decommissioning protocols for trained neural cultures, acceptable use policies restricting customer applications, or data governance frameworks. No jurisdiction currently regulates commercial biocomputing; each relevant regulatory framework (FDA HCT/P, Common Rule, ISSCR, EU Tissues and Cells Directive, Swiss HRA, UK Human Tissue Act) excludes biocomputing through a different structural mechanism. The audit further establishes that the commercial trajectory of the field (from $35,000 hardware units toward subscription-priced cloud access) is systematically dismantling the economic and logistical barriers that currently constrain who has unsupervised access to living human neural tissue.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Building on this evidentiary foundation, the paper argues that the governance conversation's near-exclusive focus on consciousness thresholds is a distraction from the more tractable (and more urgent) question: what will people do with cheap, unsupervised access to adaptive biological systems made of human neurons? Drawing on documented patterns of robot abuse, the established link between animal cruelty and interpersonal violence, and the behavioral economics of technology diffusion, the paper contends that the predictable abuse cases are not edge cases but the standard consequence of low-cost access minus observation. The absence of governance infrastructure is not merely a transparency failure; it is a structural precondition for harm at scale.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n  Supplementary evidence log available at: &lt;a href=\"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.31724218\" rel=\"nofollow\"&gt;https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.31724218&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</jats:p>",
